                                        "entry_id": "fa743b4e-eb96-39d0-9f66-822d341fd930",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A worm that lives in the ground."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "a4bed9c7-1ace-4ba8-a1b4-1603ad5ce65a",
                                                "sense_id": "df9664a6-f498-3f61-851d-35e7c144aa9c",
                                                "text": "It does not, however, happen that the whole spermatosphere is converted into spermatoblasts; there remains a passive portion, which in the earthworm occupies a central position; this is the \"sperm-blasophore,\" or \"blastophoral cell.\""
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "7a2b98ff-9bdc-3681-be24-193867366b78",
                                        "entry_id": "fa743b4e-eb96-39d0-9f66-822d341fd930",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A worm of the family Lumbricidae, or, more generally, of the suborder Lumbricina."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "ab91d401-b833-4727-ba71-60211b25704f",
                                                "sense_id": "7a2b98ff-9bdc-3681-be24-193867366b78",
                                                "text": "Before the study was begun, the known oligochaete fauna of Tasmania consisted of six species of aquatic microdriles, twenty-three indigenous species of megascolecid earthworms, and introduced earthworms of the family Lumbricidae."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
